  faqs: [{ question: "Can you tell if my pelvis is out by looking at it?", answer: "Relief Plus does not use that explanation. Visual asymmetry and palpation alone do not confirm SI-joint pain. History, test clusters, regional screening and function are considered together." }, { question: "How is SI-joint pain confirmed?", answer: "A compatible history and a cluster of provocation tests can raise clinical suspicion. In selected persistent cases, an image-guided diagnostic block may be used by an appropriate specialist when confirmation would change care." }, { question: "Can chiropractic and physical therapy work together?", answer: "Yes, when appropriate. Manual care may help selected joint or symptom findings while rehabilitation builds capacity for gait, lifting and other tasks." }, { question: "Is SI pain common in pregnancy?", answer: "Pelvic-girdle symptoms can occur during pregnancy or postpartum, but severe or atypical symptoms require appropriate obstetric or medical review. Care should avoid fear-based instability claims." }],
